  "practice_data": {
    "method_card": {
      "steps": [
        "Read the key on any pictogram before counting symbols",
        "Multiply the number of complete symbols by the key value",
        "For partial symbols, use the fraction shown (half symbol = half the key value)",
        "Check a frequency table by summing all frequencies to get the total",
        "Identify misrepresentation by checking whether the axis starts at zero and the scale is even",
        "State why the misrepresentation is misleading, not just that it exists"
      ],
      "title": "Tally Charts, Tabulation and Pictograms",
      "content": "<p>A <strong>tally chart</strong> is a quick way to count items as they occur. Each item is recorded with a vertical stroke; the fifth stroke is drawn diagonally across the previous four to form a bundle of five, making counting fast and accurate. Always group your tally marks into fives before reading totals.</p><p>A <strong>frequency table</strong> (or tabulation) organises data into categories with their corresponding frequencies. When data is numerical, it is grouped into <strong>class intervals</strong> &mdash; for example, &ldquo;10 &le; h &lt; 20&rdquo;. Each interval uses &le; on the left and &lt; on the right so that no value falls in two classes at once.</p><p>A <strong>pictogram</strong> uses a repeated symbol to represent data. The most important step is reading the <strong>key</strong> carefully: if one symbol represents 4 items, then half a symbol represents 2 items and a quarter symbol represents 1 item. The most common error at Foundation tier is ignoring the key and treating every symbol as worth 1.</p><p>When <strong>critiquing</strong> a diagram, look for these misrepresentation errors: a <strong>truncated axis</strong> (y-axis not starting at zero, which exaggerates differences), an <strong>incorrect scale</strong> (unequal gaps between tick marks), and <strong>distorted sizing</strong> (symbols at different sizes in a pictogram, or bar widths that vary). Any of these can mislead the reader about the size of differences in the data.</p><p>When choosing between formats: use a <strong>tally chart</strong> to collect live data; use a <strong>frequency table</strong> to present discrete or grouped data clearly; use a <strong>pictogram</strong> when the audience is non-specialist and the frequencies are whole multiples of the key value.</p>"
    },
